1. Detailed colonist needs: food, rest, safety, comfort, light, social contact.
2. Work priorities per colonist and per work type.
3. Passions/traits that affect speed, mood, and combat behavior.
4. Relationships: friends, rivals, family bonds, grief, romance.
5. Mental breaks: dazed wandering, tantrums, berserk panic, catharsis after recovery.
6. Proper dining areas with table/chair morale bonuses.
7. Bedrooms/habs with quality and industry-noise penalties.
8. Medical triage, tending quality, infection, prosthetics, and recovery beds.
9. Prisoner rooms and recruitment interactions.
10. Skill XP and specialization over time.
11. Outfit/equipment loadouts for work or combat roles.
12. Morale auras from gardens, lamps, memorials, mess halls, temples, libraries.
13. Social events: ceremonies, feasts, funerals, arguments, celebrations.
14. Visitors, traders, migrants, refugees, and quest pawns.
15. Assignable schedules: sleep, recreation, work, emergency shift.
16. Colonist stance defaults: flee, fight, ignore danger.
17. Rescue/carry system for downed colonists.
18. Personal vows or backstories that create mini-goals.
19. Education/training room for improving weak skills.
20. Population pressure: more mouths and bedrooms versus more labor.

